Two gates that the ordinary build cannot be: one that runs the whole tree under
AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, and one that compiles it with
Clang and libc++.  Neither of them is part of "make check" - both build a tree of
their own, because a sanitized or a libc++ object file next to an ordinary one is
a link error waiting to happen, and neither touches the checkout they are run
from.

They exist because the core overhaul is a large change to code with no test of
its own memory behaviour and one deployment target (FreeBSD) whose C++ standard
library is not the one this is developed against.  Run them before and after any
core change; a finding of theirs is worth more the day the change is made than
the week after.


test/sanitize/run.sh
----------------------
Exports the tracked working tree into a fresh directory under ${TMPDIR:-/tmp},
configures it with

	CXXFLAGS="-g -O1 -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fsanitize=address,undefined -fno-sanitize=vptr"
	LDFLAGS="-fsanitize=address,undefined"

builds it with the twelve-module list of the ordinary build, and then runs both
test suites there: "make check" and the whole integration harness of
test/harness.  The harness runs the gnuworld of the tree it sits in, which is the
sanitized one, and the daemon it spawns inherits the sanitizer options from the
script - so every line the harness sends the daemon is a sanitized line.

	test/sanitize/run.sh                       # everything
	test/sanitize/run.sh --keep                # and keep the exported tree
	test/sanitize/run.sh -- test_logging.py    # one harness file only

Everything after "--" is passed to pytest.  It must be run from the repository
root and says so if it is not (exit 2).  It needs the host toolchain (GCC with
libasan and libubsan) and, for the harness's Postgres, Docker.  A run of the
whole thing takes a good while: the sanitized build is slower than the ordinary
one, and so is every test in it.

Only tracked files are exported: an untracked build product of the host in there
would be an object file linked against the unsanitized libraries it was built
with.  Uncommitted edits to tracked files ARE included, so the gate tests what
the tree says right now.

ONE THING TO KNOW ABOUT BOTH GATES.  configure.ac assigns CXXFLAGS outright -

	CXXFLAGS="-W -Wall -pipe -I\$(top_srcdir)/libltdl"

- and so throws away whatever CXXFLAGS was passed to configure a moment before.
LDFLAGS it only ever appends to.  A gate that passes its compile flags to
configure alone therefore gets a build that is LINKED its way and COMPILED the
ordinary way, which for the sanitizers means libasan and libubsan loaded and not
one instrumented instruction: the runtime's malloc interceptors and its SIGSEGV
handler still report, so the run looks like it is working while every buffer
overflow in the tree goes unseen.  Both scripts therefore say their flags twice -
once to configure, as documentation of intent, and once on make's own command
line, where nothing can drop them, together with the value configure settled on,
because that value carries the include paths the tree needs.  The sanitizer gate
then ASSERTS the instrumentation is there ("=== Is it really instrumented?",
which counts the undefined __asan_/__ubsan_ references of
libgnuworldcore.so.0) and fails if it is not.  Fixing configure.ac would make
both of those steps unnecessary; until then, do not simplify them away.

WHAT A FAILURE LOOKS LIKE.  A sanitizer says nothing through an exit status: a
daemon the harness stops looks exactly like one ASan aborted.  So both
sanitizers are told to write their reports to files -

	ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0:abort_on_error=1:halt_on_error=1:log_path=$dir/asan:suppressions=$dir/test/sanitize/asan.supp
	UBSAN_OPTIONS=print_stacktrace=1:halt_on_error=1:log_path=$dir/ubsan

- and the script fails if any $dir/asan.* or $dir/ubsan.* file exists at the end,
whatever every step's exit status was.  It prints each of them in full under
"=== Sanitizer reports", and it keeps the exported tree when it fails, so the
report's line numbers point at files that are still there.  On success the tree
is removed; --keep keeps it either way.  Exit status: 0 only if every step passed
and neither sanitizer wrote anything, 1 otherwise, 2 if it was misused.

The two runtimes do not agree on where to write, so the script looks in two
places.  ASan honours log_path.  GCC's libubsan IGNORES it (checked, GCC 14) and
always writes to stderr, so every step's output is captured to a log in $dir and
those logs are searched for UBSan's own first line, "runtime error:", which fails
the run just as a report file does.

ONE CASE ESCAPES BOTH, and it is the one to know about: the daemon's stderr.
test/harness/gnuworld_proc.py starts gnuworld with stderr merged into the pipe it
reads for wait_for_stdout, and does not print what it collects - so a UBSan
finding inside the running daemon shows up only as a FAILING HARNESS TEST, with
no report file and nothing in any log.  To get the message out of one:

	test/sanitize/run.sh --keep -- test_logging.py
	cd <the kept directory>/test/harness
	ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0:suppressions=<kept>/test/sanitize/asan.supp \
	UBSAN_OPTIONS=print_stacktrace=1 \
	    ../../gnuworld -c -f <a conf of that test's tmp directory>

which runs the daemon with its stderr on the terminal.  pytest's own tmp
directories (/tmp/pytest-of-*/pytest-*/) still hold the GNUWorld.conf and
logging.conf of the failing test, which is the shortest way to a conf that
reproduces it.

test/sanitize/asan.supp holds the only suppressions there are, eight of them, all
one cause and all there because without them the daemon aborts before main() in
every harness test: libgnuworld.a is linked into both the gnuworld executable and
libgnuworldcore.so, so every global of libgnuworld exists twice and ASan's ODR
checker says so.  Read the file - it names each one and says what the duplication
costs.  They are a pre-existing finding waiting for the task that fixes the
linkage, not a dismissal, and the check itself stays on: an ODR violation the
overhaul introduces still fails the gate.  Nothing that does not stop the gate
running belongs in that file.

-fno-sanitize=vptr is the one check of the twenty-odd in "undefined" that this
tree cannot be built with, and it costs a link rather than producing a report:
the vptr check emits a reference to the typeinfo of every polymorphic type a
pointer is used through, and libgnuworld/match.cc takes a "const iClient*" - a
type of the CORE, whose typeinfo lives in libgnuworldcore.  test_burst and
test_mtrie_load link libgnuworld alone, deliberately, so with the check on they
end in "undefined reference to `typeinfo for gnuworld::iClient'" and nothing at
all gets built.  Take it out the day libgnuworld stops naming a core type; do not
widen it to anything else.

detect_leaks=0 IS DELIBERATE, for now.  A survey of core found leaks that are
still there at exit - objects the daemon allocates once and never gives back -
and later parts of the core overhaul are what fix them.  Leak checking today
would bury every real finding under them, so the gate watches for the errors that
are bugs wherever they happen (buffer overflows, use-after-free, undefined
behaviour) and leaves the leaks to the tasks that own them.  Turn it to 1 once
those are done; that is a one-word change in the script.


test/docker/clang-libcxx/run.sh
---------------------------------
A COMPILE gate: it builds the image of the Dockerfile beside it (Debian trixie,
Clang and libc++, named gnuworld-clang-libcxx), streams the tracked working tree
into a container of it through a tar on stdin, and there configures with

	CXX=clang++ CXXFLAGS="-stdlib=libc++" LDFLAGS="-stdlib=libc++"

builds, asserts that the daemon really came out linked against libc++ and not
libstdc++, and runs "make check".

	test/docker/clang-libcxx/run.sh

It must be run from the repository root too (exit 2), and it needs Docker and
nothing else.  Nothing is mounted and nothing it writes reaches the host: when it
is done the only thing left behind is the image.

What it is for: FreeBSD is a deployment target and its C++ standard library is
libc++, while the machine this is usually developed on has libstdc++ alone - so a
difference between the two is invisible here until it is a bug report.  It has
happened.  Code once compared the ADDRESS of std::endl to decide whether a
manipulator was the end of a line: libstdc++ gave one address per program and the
comparison worked, libc++ instantiates the template per translation unit, so the
addresses differed and the comparison silently never matched.  It compiled
cleanly on both.

The image deliberately has NO prometheus-cpp, NO log4cplus and NO libcurl.  All
three are C++ libraries, and the Debian ones are built against libstdc++: linking
them into a libc++ program mixes two incompatible std:: ABIs.  Nothing had to be
dropped from the module list for that - each of the three is reached only through
ENABLE_LOG4CPLUS, HAVE_PROMETHEUS or HAVE_LIBCURL, which configure does not
define in this image, and mod.dronescan, mod.cservice and libnotifier all compile
without them.  The code behind those three is what test/docker/full-deps compiles
instead, with the other standard library.

WHAT A FAILURE LOOKS LIKE.  Warnings are counted and printed but are not fatal:
Clang warns about things GCC does not, and a gate that failed on every one of
those would be turned off in a week.  The summary line says how many there were,
and the kinds are listed under "-W" names.  What fails it is an error: a compile
error (the first forty "error:" lines are printed, then the tail of the build
log), a daemon linked against libstdc++ after all, or a failing "make check" (the
FAIL: block of the log is printed).  It ends in either "ALL GOOD" or "SOMETHING
FAILED, see above", and exits 0 or 1 to match.


Proving a gate still works
----------------------------
Both gates build a tree that is not the checkout, which makes them easy to check
for real: plant the fault in the exported tree rather than in the repository.
Run the sanitizer gate once with --keep, write a heap-buffer-overflow into a core
.cc file of the directory it kept, and run make and the harness there - the ASan
report appears as an asan.* file beside it.  For the Clang gate, a syntax error
in any file of the tree before it is streamed in is enough; the tar is made from
git's file list, so the fault has to be in a tracked file, and a copy of the
checkout is the place to do that.
